  "findings": [
    "Both fixtures execute MAIN BurnGetHandle from the original 192-byte update header and then invoke original MAIN Burn with its observed caller arguments.",
    "The native-open fixture follows original installed-media FileX opening and driver initialization to the first observed NAND ID request, op 2, at FcuNandOperationEx entry after 281 VEX block events. No Ex response is supplied.",
    "The separate installed-header fixture models FileX open and geometry. Native HostAck and BurnNand reach the ordinary installed-header loader's op 6 request after 134 VEX block events. Modeled op 6 and op 4 responses allow original card Read to reach its first FileX read entry after 156 cumulative events.",
    "The installed-header fixture borrows 0x600 bytes from original package offset 0x1000c0 for the modeled op 4 response. The card FileX entry requests 0x20000 bytes in the primary buffer. Its intended payload offset is setup provenance. The FILE cursor and read data are unobserved."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "The two runs use separate fresh projects, seeded mutex and device state, and bounded read/write regions. Observations use VEX block history counts from the MAIN Burn entry.",
    "MAIN DownloadCard's caller arguments are verified in the prior update-service report. Neither fixture begins at DownloadCard or exercises actual storage, card I/O or NAND controller behavior.",
    "The installed-media header is synthetic, and selector 1 is beyond the stop. No Ex function body, program operation, continuation, update acceptance, reboot or hardware outcome is verified.",
    "The MAIN and E01 aligned code measurements establish structural similarity. E01 replay results apply to the E01 implementation."
  ],
  "next_actions": [
    "Replay MAIN selector 1 and section-map construction with separately controlled installed headers and geometry.",
    "Test MAIN card-read retry and byte comparison separately from a bounded later MAIN program request."
  ],
